      ":question/math-academy-id": "e-3655",
      ":question/problem": "Given $y = \\dfrac {3 x^2 + x - 1} x$, find $\\dfrac {\\textrm d^{3} y} {\\textrm d x^3}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, notice that we can rewrite the function as the sum of powers of $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}+x-1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{x}+\\frac{x}{x}-\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =3x+1-{x}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x+1-{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =3+0-(-1){x}^{-2} \\\\  & =3+{x}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3+{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =0+(-2){x}^{-3} \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =-2(-3){x}^{-4} \\\\  & =6{x}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
